{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgdTG64HFWazkfmNJgFAB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01UAKEB7f8ZwBEncFgJxJxpn","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"status":"partial","summary":"The feature is in place and its tests pass. The only open point: the full test suite was still running when I had to answer. About 1,000 tests had finished with no failures or errors (only skips), but the run was not complete.\n\nWhat changed:\n- `Redmine::Search::Tokenizer` now has a `MAX_TOKENS = 5` constant. `tokens` still returns at most 5 terms, and the new `truncated?` method says when the text had more. Repeated terms and terms that are too short are removed before counting.\n- `Query#filter_warnings` returns one message per filter whose value was cut. It only looks at filters of type string, text or search with the operators `~`, `*~`, `!~`, `^` and `$`. Warnings do not make the query invalid.\n- A new helper, `query_filter_warnings`, shows each message as `<p class=\"warning\">`. Rails escapes the terms. It is called right after the error messages in the shared query form (issues, projects, users, admin projects, time entries), the calendar and the Gantt chart, and the results are still displayed.\n\nYour task text was garbled in the operator list: the last operator was replaced by a copy of the prompt. I assumed it was `$` (\"ends with\"), the only other operator that splits the value into terms.","changes":[{"path":"lib/redmine/search.rb","summary":"Tokenizer: added the MAX_TOKENS = 5 constant, the truncated? method, and a cached private all_tokens; tokens returns the first MAX_TOKENS."},{"path":"app/models/query.rb","summary":"Added filter_warnings, which builds one message per string/text/search filter with operator ~, *~, !~, ^ or $ whose value was cut."},{"path":"app/helpers/queries_helper.rb","summary":"Added query_filter_warnings(query), which shows each warning as an HTML-escaped <p class=\"